            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.488,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.204
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Consider the sequence 1, 4, 7, 10, 13, … Can you predict the next four terms? Can you derive the first 10 terms of the sequence obtained by adding all the terms up to a given term of this sequence? ("
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The first term is 1. The second term is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 + 4 = 5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , the third term is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1+4+7=12"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and so on.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.204,
            0.886,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In some sequences (like the ones described till now), the terms follow a certain pattern, a rule or order. To describe these rules it is helpful to have a convenient notation to describe a sequence. We can use "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { 1 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " to represent the first term of a sequence, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " to represent the second term, and so on. In this notation, the subscripts match the term numbers. Thus, for the sequence of odd numbers, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t_{1} = 1, t_{2} = 3, t_{3} = 5, t_{4} = 7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and so on. This notation helps to connect the position of the term to the actual term. For example, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { 4 } = 7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " tells us that the term in the fourth position is 7. We may need to talk about more than one sequence at a time. We can use different letters for these. Hence, we can use "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { _ 1 } , t _ { _ 2 } , t _ { _ 3 } , \\ldots"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for one sequence, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\mathcal { S } } _ { 1 } , { \\mathcal { S } } _ { 2 } , { \\mathcal { S } } _ { 3 } , \\ldots"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for another one, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "u _ { 1 } , u _ { 2 } , u _ { 3 } , \\ldots"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for a third sequence, and so on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.436,
            0.884,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Can you write "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { 5 } , t _ { 6 } , t _ { 7 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { t _ { g } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for the sequence of triangular numbers?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.475,
            0.886,
            0.571
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There can be many kinds of sequences. For example, the terms could be fractions or negative integers. The ones we have discussed so far have terms that are increasing. But there could be sequences such as 1, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\textstyle { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } , { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } } , { \\frac { 1 } { 4 } } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " … where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t_{1} = 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and the following terms are unit fractions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.572,
            0.886,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "occurring in decreasing order. We can also have sequences such as –7, –3, 1, 5, 9, … where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "s _ { _ 1 } = - 7 ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and consecutive terms have a difference of 4. Note that when we use the notation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { n }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for describing a term, n is always a non-negative integer but the term itself can be negative, or in fact any real number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.696,
            0.398,
            0.719
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.732,
            0.855,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can you think of any other kinds of sequences? List out five different types of sequences and discuss their properties with your friends."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.812,
            0.585,
            0.835
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.2 Explicit Rule for a Sequence"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.846,
            0.886,
            0.906
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using the notation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { n }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (or "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "s _ { n }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "u _ { n } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " we can write an explicit rule for the term in the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " position of a sequence, that is, the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "t",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "erm. An explicit "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "formula",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " uses the term’s position number, n, to calculate its value."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
